/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Model the receiver as a band-pass filter of the transmission bandwidth, a detector, and a low-pass filter of the message bandwidth W.
   - Let P be the average received signal power in watts, carrier and sidebands together.
   - both ratios as powers, for the same P, N0 and W; FOM above 1 means the scheme does better than plain baseband, below 1 worse.
   - Some books measure the FM input noise in the transmission bandwidth BT instead of W.
   - AM with an envelope detector: the signal is Ac[1 + m cos 2πfm t] cos 2πfc t, with total power P = (Ac²/2)(1 + m²/2).
   - m is the modulation index, 0 ≤ m ≤ 1; the largest value, at m = 1, is 1/3 (−4.77 dB).
   - The carrier takes power without carrying the message, and that is the whole loss.
   - DSB-SC with coherent detection: the signal Ac m(t) cos 2πfc t has power Ac²Pm/2, where Pm is the message power.
   - SSB with coherent detection: the receiver filter is only W wide, so half the noise enters, but the detected message power is also halved.
   - With a strong carrier the noise mainly tilts the carrier phasor; the phase error is about ns(t)/Ac.
*/
