/* book Worked example: Q: An AM receiver with m = 0.5 and an FM receiver with β = 5 receive the same power with the same noise, giving a channel SNR of 20 dB in the message bandwidth W = 5 kHz. Find each FOM, each output SNR and each transmission bandwidth.
   1. Channel SNR 20 dB = 100.
   2. AM: FOM = 0.25/2.25 = 0.1111 = −9.54 dB, so (S/N)o = 20 − 9.54 = 10.46 dB.
   3. FM: FOM = 1.5 × 25 = 37.5 = 15.74 dB, so (S/N)o = 20 + 15.74 = 35.74 dB.
   4. Bandwidths: AM 2W = 10 kHz; FM 2(5 + 1) × 5 = 60 kHz.
   5. FM is 25.28 dB better for six times the bandwidth (Figure 3.5.1).
   Answer: AM: FOM 0.111 (−9.54 dB), output 10.46 dB, 10 kHz. FM: FOM 37.5 (15.74 dB), output 35.74 dB, 60 kHz. */
