/* book Worked example 2: Q: A receiver needs an output SNR of 40 dB for a W = 15 kHz message. The noise is N0 = kT0 = 4 × 10⁻²¹ W/Hz. Find the received power needed with AM (m = 1), DSB-SC, SSB and FM (β = 5), and check that the FM carrier is above a 10 dB threshold in its Carson bandwidth.
   1. Noise in the message band: N0W = 4 × 10⁻²¹ × 15 000 = 6 × 10⁻¹⁷ W.
   2. Needed (S/N)c = (S/N)o/FOM, so P = 10⁴ × 6 × 10⁻¹⁷/FOM = 6 × 10⁻¹³/FOM W.
   3. AM: P = 6 × 10⁻¹³ × 3 = 1.8 × 10⁻¹² W = −87.4 dBm.
   4. DSB-SC and SSB: P = 6 × 10⁻¹³ W = −92.2 dBm.
   5. FM: P = 6 × 10⁻¹³/37.5 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁴ W = −108.0 dBm.
   6. Threshold check: BT = 2(5 + 1) × 15 = 180 kHz; noise in BT = 4 × 10⁻²¹ × 180 000 = 7.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ W; carrier-to-noise ratio = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁴/7.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ = 22.2 = 13.5 dB, above 10 dB, so the FM figure holds.
   Answer: AM −87.4 dBm, DSB-SC and SSB −92.2 dBm, FM −108.0 dBm (20.5 dB less than AM); the FM carrier is 13.5 dB above the noise in 180 kHz, so it is above threshold. */
