/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Measure the received carrier against the noise in the IF bandwidth BT: the carrier-to-noise ratio ρ = C/(N0BT).
   - ρ = C/(N0BT) is the carrier-to-noise ratio in BT = 2(β + 1)W; for β = 5 the factor 3β²(β + 1) = 450, a 26.5 dB improvement.
   - This is the (3/2)β² of the figure of merit rewritten with the noise in BT.
   - The threshold is usually defined as the ρ at which the output SNR is 1 dB below the straight line.
   - Figure 3.6.1 draws the straight-line law for the two receivers of the worked examples against received carrier power.
   - Threshold extension moves the knee to a lower ρ.
   - When two FM signals share a channel, the limiter sees their sum.
   - This helps FM broadcasting: a distant co-channel station is weaker at your receiver, so it is captured out, with far less co-channel interference than AM would suffer.
*/
